====================================================================================================
v125  ARBITRARY DATA IN THE REGISTER:  what qlto_qml can hold, and the price
====================================================================================================
  qlto_qml's three circuits are flat in |D|. This asks what DATASET fits
  in the register at all, which is the question that decides application.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 1  what can the linear encoder represent?   NO CIRCUIT (tier C)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ang_j(x) = sum_d alpha[j,d]*bit_d(x) is linear in the register bits, so
  the reachable angle tables form a d-dimensional subspace of R^(2^d).
  Least-squares fit of alpha to a random target table, per sample size:

      d   |D|   free params   rel. residual   variance captured
   --------------------------------------------------------------------
      2     4         6          0.7719           40.4%
      3     8         9          0.7387           45.4%
      4    16        12          0.8593           26.2%
      5    32        15          0.9362           12.4%

  The linear encoder holds n_sys*d numbers and an arbitrary table needs
  n_sys*2^d, so the residual grows with d and the register is a
  STRUCTURED-DATA device as built. It cannot load a general dataset.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 2  does the mux encoder actually load an arbitrary table?   tier B
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Exact amplitudes. Compare f_x read out of the register circuit against
  f_x from directly preparing sample x's angles. Exactness question, so
  no shots and no cost figure here.

      d   |D|   max |f_reg - f_direct|   verdict
   ------------------------------------------------------------
      2     4          2.22e-16            ok
      3     8          7.22e-16            ok
      4    16          7.77e-16            ok

   PASS - an arbitrary dataset IS loadable. The rest is what it costs.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 3  the price, on transpiled circuits   tier A
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Transpiled to ['rz', 'sx', 'x', 'cx']. 'circuits' is counted from q.ncircuits, not asserted.

      d   |D|    encoder     gates    2q gates   depth   circuits   cos
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      2     4   linear         92         14       42       3      +0.9985
      2     4   arbitrary      92         14       52       3      +0.9884

      3     8   linear        129         24       55       3      +0.9356
      3     8   arbitrary     159         30      115       3      +0.8225

      4    16   linear        170         38       73       3      +0.9851
      4    16   arbitrary     290         62      243       3      +0.9931

      5    32   linear        219         60       99       3      +0.9635
      5    32   arbitrary     549        126      499       3      +0.9384

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 4  WHICH BLOCK IS THE COST?  prep vs encode, counted separately
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  PART 3's 'linear' column fits a slope of 0.70 in d, which is NOT the
  n_sys*d the linear encoder should cost. Something else in the circuit is
  growing with |D|, so the two blocks are counted apart here rather than
  attributed by argument.

      d   |D|   prep 2q   linear enc 2q   arbitrary enc 2q   prep share
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      2     4        2            12                 12         14.3%
      3     8        6            18                 24         25.0%
      4    16       14            24                 48         36.8%
      5    32       30            30                 96         50.0%

  Fitted slope of log2(2q gates) against d:
      weighted state prep   1.29   -> ~ |D|^1.29
      linear encoder        0.44   -> ~ |D|^0.44

====================================================================================================
READING IT
====================================================================================================
  CIRCUIT COUNT IS UNCHANGED at three for both encoders at every size -
  the flat-in-|D| claim in qlto_qml survives arbitrary data intact.

  GATE COUNT DOES NOT. Two-qubit gates, arbitrary over linear:

      |D|     linear 2q    arbitrary 2q    ratio
   --------------------------------------------------------
        4          14              14         1.0x
        8          24              30         1.2x
       16          38              62         1.6x
       32          60             126         2.1x

  Fitted slope of log2(WHOLE-CIRCUIT 2q gates) against d = log2|D|:
      with linear encoder     0.70
      with arbitrary encoder  1.06

  Both are near or above 1.0, so the FULL circuit is linear in |D| either
  way - even with the cheap encoder.

  AND THE ENCODER WAS THE WRONG SUSPECT FOR IT. PART 4 counts the blocks
  apart: the linear encoder scales as |D|^0.44, logarithmic as designed,
  while THE WEIGHTED STATE PREP scales as |D|^1.29 and reaches 50% of the
  two-qubit gates by |D|=32, still climbing. Reading PART 3's 0.70 as
  'the linear encoder is cheap' would have been wrong in a specific way:
  the encoder IS cheap, and the circuit is not.

  THAT CANNOT BE FIXED BY CHOOSING A BETTER DATASET. qlto_qml's premise is
  p_x proportional to |w_x| - an ARBITRARY distribution that changes every
  epoch. Setting 2^d - 1 free amplitudes needs at least 2^d - 1 angles, so
  the prep is Theta(|D|) by parameter counting, not by any weakness of the
  Gray-code construction. The weighted register is inherently linear in |D|
  in GATES however structured the data is. Structure buys the encoder; it
  does not buy the prep.

  Loading 1024 arbitrary samples extrapolates to ~4881 two-qubit gates by the
  arbitrary-encoder fit, which no NISQ device runs. That is the QRAM wall;
  this file locates it rather than moving it.

  ON THE DATASET RESTRICTION ITSELF. PART 1 shows the linear encoder
  captures only a d-dimensional subspace of a 2^d-dimensional angle table -
  40%, 45%, 26%, 12% of the variance of a random target at d = 2..5, falling
  as d grows. So it is a STRUCTURED-DATA device: it fits samples generated
  by a few underlying factors - parameter sweeps, designed experiments,
  lattices of settings - and not unstructured data. That restriction is real
  and is separate from the gate-count question PART 4 takes up.

  SO WHAT IS THE MODULE ACTUALLY FOR, given both findings. Three circuits
  per epoch against parameter-shift's 2M|D| is a real and large saving in
  CIRCUIT COUNT - the quantity that carries per-job latency, queue time and
  compilation overhead on real hardware. It is not a saving in depth, and
  the Theta(|D|) prep means |D| is bounded by coherence, not by the method.
  The regime where that trade is favourable is: modest |D| (tens), a system
  register small enough that the prep fits in coherence, structured samples
  so the cheap encoder suffices, and a workload where job count rather than
  circuit depth is the binding constraint. Sweeps and designed experiments
  sit in that regime. Unstructured large-batch learning does not.

  SCOPE. N_sys=3, |D| <= 32, efficient_su2 reps=1, 32768 shots, one seed per
  cell, no noise model, no hardware, single-Pauli readout so G=1. The
  slope fits use four points over one decade and are trends, not laws. No
  claim here about whether a NOISY device preserves the cos column - that
  is untested for this module and is the gap that most limits application.
